//? Title: Clustering Iris: Centroids, Merge Trees and Density
//? Subtitle: Exact small examples before comparing three definitions of a group
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given only measurements, we want groups of similar observations without prescribing
species labels as a training target. Similarity alone does not specify a unique
partition: distance to a centre, the cost of a merge and local density answer different
questions. Four collinear points and two short density chains make those mechanisms
checkable by hand. We then cluster all four Iris measurements, compare K-means cluster
counts and restarts, re-cut a hierarchical tree, and vary DBSCAN's radius. Species
labels describe the fitted results afterwards. Explicit noise coverage and
permutation-invariant agreement prevent purity from being mistaken for predictive
accuracy or evidence for a uniquely correct cluster count.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Specify a geometry and a question

An observation is a numeric vector \(x_i\in\mathbb R^p\). Every fit below uses
Euclidean distance in the supplied feature units. Neither the clustering classes nor
these diagnostics standardize features automatically. Changing a single column's units
changes the geometry; equal units do not imply equal variance or scientific importance.
Uniform scaling by a multiplies squared-distance objectives by a squared, and preserving
DBSCAN neighbourhoods then requires scaling epsilon by the absolute value of a.

The concept diagram contrasts three rules for forming a group. The retained TikZ
source is rendered locally using LaTeX with PGF/TikZ, standalone and Latin Modern,
then dvisvgm.
Those tools are required only when regenerating the diagram; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/clustering_iris_concepts.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/clustering_iris_concepts.tikz > build/clustering_iris_concepts.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/clustering_iris_concepts.svg build/clustering_iris_concepts.dvi > build/clustering_iris_concepts.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local clustering concept diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local clustering concept diagram.
// \image(figures/clustering_iris_concepts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Centre assignments, nested merges and density connectivity define different kinds of clusters.)
\alttext(Three TikZ panels show points assigned to two centres, four leaves combined into a merge tree, and a DBSCAN core with two border neighbours plus a separate noise point. Only the core expands a density-connected group.)

>> Lloyd updates and careful seeding

For k nonempty groups, K-means minimizes the within-cluster sum of squares (WCSS),
also called inertia. Holding centres fixed gives nearest-centre assignments; holding
assignments fixed gives means:

$$
 J=\sum_i\|x_i-\mu_{z_i}\|^2,\qquad
 z_i\leftarrow\arg\min_c\|x_i-\mu_c\|^2,\qquad
 \mu_c\leftarrow\frac1{|C_c|}\sum_{i\in C_c}x_i.
$$
\alttext(K-means sums squared distances to assigned centres, assigns each point to its nearest centre, and recomputes each nonempty centre as a mean.)

The derivative of a group's squared-error sum is
\(2|C_c|\mu_c-2\sum_{i\in C_c}x_i\), so the mean minimizes that subproblem.
Each exact assignment and nonempty mean update cannot increase J. This is alternating
minimization, with no guarantee of a global minimum. The implementation reseeds an
empty centre at a farthest point. It stops when every centre's Euclidean shift is below
tolerance, or at the iteration cap, then reassigns labels to the final centres.
At a capped, unconverged fit those centres need not equal the final assigned-group means.

K-means++ chooses the first centre uniformly and each later point with probability
\(D_i^2/\sum_jD_j^2\), where D is distance to the nearest existing centre.
The native implementation uses that single-draw seeding, then keeps the lowest-inertia
run across `n_init` restarts. We use positive iteration/restart budgets and a fixed seed;
`iterations_used()` counts only the winning run. Extending the restart count at the
same seed keeps the earlier candidates, so the best objective cannot worsen. Increasing
k makes the globally optimal objective nonincreasing, but independently initialized
finite local fits have no such general guarantee. An elbow is descriptive, not a
validated estimator of a biological species count.

>> Linkage and the units of a merge height

Agglomeration starts from singleton groups. Single linkage takes the minimum
cross-group distance; complete takes the maximum; average takes the mean over all
cross-group point pairs. Ward instead minimizes the increase in WCSS:

$$
 \Delta(A,B)=\frac{|A||B|}{|A|+|B|}\|\mu_A-\mu_B\|^2,\qquad
 h_{\mathrm{Ward}}(A,B)=\sqrt{2\Delta(A,B)}.
$$
\alttext(Ward's squared-error increase is the size-weighted squared distance between the two means. The native reported merge height is the square root of twice that increase.)

The identity follows by splitting each squared distance about its old and merged
means; the centred cross terms vanish. The native Lance-Williams update keeps squared
Ward distances internally and reports their square roots. Thus its height is not the
raw SSE increase. Ward requires Euclidean distance. Every fit builds all n−1 merges;
`cut(k)` replays a prefix of that same tree, so changing the cut needs no refit.
Tied merges may produce different valid trees in another implementation.

>> Core connectivity, borders and noise

DBSCAN uses a closed radius neighbourhood that includes the point itself:

$$
 N_\varepsilon(i)=\{j:\|x_i-x_j\|\leq\varepsilon\},\qquad
 i\text{ is core}\iff |N_\varepsilon(i)|\geq m.
$$
\alttext(A point is core if its closed epsilon neighbourhood, counting itself, contains at least the chosen minimum number of samples.)

Core-to-core chains form components. Noncore neighbours of a core join as border
points but cannot extend a chain; everything else is noise, labelled -1. A point
initially marked noise may later be reclaimed as a border. If a border touches two
components its assignment can depend on traversal order. Epsilon and min_samples,
as well as feature scaling and distance choice, define the result. More epsilon can
create core components before it joins them, so cluster count need not be monotone.
A two-cluster result at one radius does not establish two intrinsic populations.

>> Agreement needs both a partition and coverage

With a contingency table \(n_{cj}\) between fitted clusters and reference classes,
assigned purity is \(\sum_{c\geq0}\max_j n_{cj}/n_{\mathrm{assigned}}\).
It ignores noise and reaches one for singleton clusters, so it rewards fragmentation
and can hide rejected observations. Always report coverage, assigned/n, beside it.
For adjusted Rand, let A and B be sums of unordered within-cluster and within-class
pair counts, and S the sum within contingency cells:

$$
 \mathrm{ARI}=\frac{S-AB/\binom n2}{(A+B)/2-AB/\binom n2},\quad
 S=\sum_{cj}\binom{n_{cj}}2,\quad A=\sum_c\binom{n_c}2,\quad B=\sum_j\binom{n_j}2.
$$
\alttext(Adjusted Rand subtracts the fixed-marginal expected same-group pair count from observed agreement and divides by its maximum possible excess.)

ARI is invariant to label renaming, equals one for identical partitions, and can be
negative. Its chance correction uses a fixed-marginal permutation model, not a
significance test. Here ARI includes all observations, treating every noise label as
one category; it does not credit noise rows as assigned. Degenerate identical partitions
receive one. Purity is NaN when all rows are noise. Neither statistic is held-out
classification accuracy, and looking at these scores to choose settings would use the
species labels for selection. We present predeclared settings and sensitivity ranges.

For n rows, p features, k centres, r restarts and T iterations, Lloyd fitting costs
O(r T n k p). This implementation's dense hierarchical search uses O(n^3+n^2 p) time
and O(n^2) memory. DBSCAN precomputes all pair distances, requiring O(n^2 p) time and
O(n^2) memory; it does not use a spatial index. External count summaries use O(n log n)
time and O(n) storage. These examples concern small data, not large-scale benchmarks.

> Simple examples

>> Four points determine the centres and Ward increments

For x=(0,2,8,10), k=2 yields means (1,9) and WCSS=4.
One mean at five gives WCSS=68. Ward first merges each adjacent pair, adding two
units of WCSS each time, then merges the two size-two groups at cost 64.
Its reported heights are (2,2,sqrt(128)); summing height squared over two recovers 68.
The table exposes every merge, including original-point ids 0..3 and merged ids 4,5.
The centre plot uses an extra zero-valued vertical coordinate only for display.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/clustering_iris_four_points.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::stats;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{0, 2, 8, 10});
  d.add_column("display_y", std::vector<double>(4, 0));
  KMeansOptions o;
  o.n_clusters = 2;
  const KMeans                  fit(d, {"x"}, o);
  const AgglomerativeClustering tree(d, {"x"});
  std::vector<int>              labels(fit.labels().begin(), fit.labels().end());
  auto centres = plot_clusters(d, "x", "display_y", labels, "K-means: means at one and nine");
  centres
      .points(
          {fit.cluster_centers()(0, 0), fit.cluster_centers()(1, 0)},
          {0, 0},
          "centres",
          {17, 24, 39},
          7)
      .y_limits(-.5, 1.5);
  auto                   dendrogram = plot_dendrogram(tree, "Ward heights encode merge costs");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  std::vector<double>    step, a, b, height, delta, total;
  double                 sum = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < tree.merge_history().size(); ++i) {
    const auto& m = tree.merge_history()[i];
    step.push_back(i);
    a.push_back(m.cluster_a);
    b.push_back(m.cluster_b);
    height.push_back(m.distance);
    delta.push_back(m.distance * m.distance / 2);
    sum += delta.back();
    total.push_back(sum);
  }
  if (std::abs(fit.inertia() - 4) > 1e-12 || std::abs(sum - 68) > 1e-12)
    throw std::runtime_error("four-point reference mismatch");
  t.add_column("merge", step);
  t.add_column("child_a", a);
  t.add_column("child_b", b);
  t.add_column("height", height);
  t.add_column("sse_increase", delta);
  t.add_column("cumulative_sse", total);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(centres)
      .add(dendrogram)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/clustering_iris_four_points.svg");
  std::print("K-means WCSS: {:.6f}.\n\n{}\n", fit.inertia(), t.to_md(6));
}
```
