| linkage | clusters | purity | adjusted_rand |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| single | 3 | 0.680000 | 0.563751 |
| complete | 3 | 0.840000 | 0.642251 |
| average | 3 | 0.906667 | 0.759199 |
| ward | 3 | 0.893333 | 0.731199 |
| ward recut | 2 | 0.666667 | 0.568116 |


Ward merge history size: 149 (unchanged by cut).
\image(figures/clustering_iris_linkage.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A common feature space supports four linkage comparisons; one retained tree supports several cuts.)
\alttext(A full Ward dendrogram and a two-cluster petal projection share one fit. A table compares three-cluster single, complete, average and Ward partitions with the two-cluster Ward cut.)

>> DBSCAN radius changes assignment as well as cluster count

The baseline uses epsilon=.6 cm in the four-dimensional Euclidean metric and
min_samples=5, including self. Grey points are unassigned noise, not a third fitted
cluster. The radius grid .2,.3,.4,.6,.8,1,1.5 is predeclared. Its table reports both
cluster count and noise/coverage, assigned purity and all-row ARI. Comparing purity
without the changing coverage would compare different selected subsets. At a fixed
min_samples, more radius can create groups before joining them. These parameter-specific
partitions do not prove how many biological populations exist, and grey points need
not lie between groups in this two-coordinate projection.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/clustering_iris_dbscan.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::stats;
  const auto                     d = dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> f = {"Sepal.Length", "Sepal.Width", "Petal.Length", "Petal.Width"};
  std::vector<std::string>       truth;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < d.nrows(); ++i)
    truth.push_back(d.string_at("Species", i));
  DBSCANOptions o;
  o.eps         = .6;
  o.min_samples = 5;
  const DBSCAN baseline(d, f, o);
  auto         projection = plot_clusters(
      d, "Petal.Length", "Petal.Width", baseline.labels(), "DBSCAN: radius 0.6, minimum five");
  std::vector<double> eps = {.2, .3, .4, .6, .8, 1, 1.5}, clusters, noise, coverage, purity, ari;
  for (double radius : eps) {
    o.eps = radius;
    const DBSCAN m(d, f, o);
    const auto   a = cluster_agreement(m.labels(), truth);
    clusters.push_back(a.clusters);
    noise.push_back(a.noise);
    coverage.push_back(a.coverage);
    purity.push_back(a.purity);
    ari.push_back(a.adjusted_rand);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("epsilon", eps);
  t.add_column("clusters", clusters);
  t.add_column("noise", noise);
  t.add_column("coverage", coverage);
  t.add_column("assigned_purity", purity);
  t.add_column("ari_including_noise", ari);
  auto sweep = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  sweep.line(eps, clusters, "clusters", {37, 99, 235})
      .points(eps, clusters, "counts", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .title("Cluster count is not monotone in radius")
      .x_label("epsilon in raw-feature distance units")
      .y_label("non-noise clusters")
      .y_limits(0, 6);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(projection)
      .add(sweep)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/clustering_iris_dbscan.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\nBaseline composition (noise row included descriptively):\n\n{}\n",
      t.to_md(6),
      cluster_composition(baseline.labels(), truth).to_md(6));
}
```
