\image(figures/clustering_iris_dbscan.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Coverage changes with the density threshold; a radius-specific two-group partition is not a universal population count.)
\alttext(The left petal projection shows baseline DBSCAN groups and grey noise. The right shows the number of non-noise clusters across seven radii. Tables report rejected counts, coverage, agreement and baseline majority composition.)

Epsilon is a hard threshold. Some Iris pair distances lie at .3 within floating-point
error. Direct coordinate-difference distances (used here) and dot-product-based
distance evaluation can put them on opposite sides of that threshold. Our independent
check against scikit-learn agrees on all seven partitions when supplied the same
explicit distance matrix; allowing it to compute distances independently changes the
partition at .3. Use a common distance matrix to isolate algorithm differences from
numerical threshold effects, and examine nearby radii when an edge is borderline.

> Conclusion

At k=3, K-means gives WCSS 78.8514, assigned purity .8933 and ARI .7302.
Average linkage has ARI .7592, compared with Ward .7312, complete .6423 and single
.5638 for these three-cluster fits. At epsilon=.6, DBSCAN gives two groups and nine
noise rows: coverage .94 and assigned purity .6738. These are post-fit descriptions,
not labels used to select an algorithm. The twenty-run K-means prefix happens to
find its best objective in the first run; that observation is specific to seed 42.

Centroid error, linkage cost and density connectivity define different partitions.
The small examples verify means and WCSS, Ward's square-root height convention, core
and border roles, and the limits of purity. Iris supplies an exploratory comparison
in one declared raw-feature geometry. Its measured WCSS, merge tree, radius sensitivity
and species agreement characterize those fits; they do not infer a unique intrinsic
cluster count, validate a predictive classifier or make every noise point an outlier
in biological terms. Feature scaling and domain purpose remain part of the question.

> References

- Lloyd, S. P. (1982). [Least Squares Quantization in PCM|https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee398a/handouts/papers/Lloyd%20-%20Least%20Squares%20Q%20in%20PCM.pdf]. IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 28(2), 129–137.
- Arthur, D. and Vassilvitskii, S. (2007). [k-means++: The Advantages of Careful Seeding|https://theory.stanford.edu/~sergei/papers/kMeansPP-soda.pdf]. SODA, 1027–1035.
- Ward, J. H. (1963). [Hierarchical Grouping to Optimize an Objective Function|https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1963.10500845]. JASA 58(301), 236–244. The report explicitly states the native square-root height convention.
- Ester, M., Kriegel, H.-P., Sander, J. and Xu, X. (1996). [A Density-Based Algorithm for Discovering Clusters in Large Spatial Databases with Noise|https://cdn.aaai.org/KDD/1996/KDD96-037.pdf]. KDD, 226–231.
- Hubert, L. and Arabie, P. (1985). [Comparing partitions|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01908075]. Journal of Classification 2, 193–218.
- Manning, C. D., Raghavan, P. and Schütze, H. (2008). [Evaluation of clustering|https://nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/html/htmledition/evaluation-of-clustering-1.html]. Introduction to Information Retrieval. Purity's sensitivity to cluster count.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. Annals of Eugenics 7(2), 179–188. Iris measurement provenance.
